3. What is systemctl?
systemctl is the command used to communicate with systemd and manage services.
Important commands
Command	Purpose
systemctl status ssh	Check SSH status
sudo systemctl start ssh	Start SSH
sudo systemctl stop ssh	Stop SSH
sudo systemctl restart ssh	Restart SSH
sudo systemctl reload ssh	Reload configuration, if supported
sudo systemctl enable ssh	Start SSH automatically at boot
sudo systemctl disable ssh	Disable automatic startup
systemctl is-active ssh	Check whether service is active
systemctl is-enabled ssh	Check boot startup setting

Important difference:
- start starts a service now.
- enable configures it to start automatically during boot.
- restart stops and starts it again.
Practical 1 – Check SSH service
Run in Ubuntu:
systemctl status ssh


If the service exists, you may see:
Active: active (running)


Other possible states include:
- inactive – service is not running.
- failed – service failed to start.
- not-found – service unit does not exist.
Don't worry if SSH is not installed on your VM. You can still practise the other commands using existing services.
Practical 2 – List running services
systemctl list-units --type=service


To see all service units, including inactive ones:
systemctl list-units --type=service --all


4. What are Linux logs?
Logs are records of events that happen inside a Linux system.

For example:
- Service started or stopped.
- Login attempts.
- Application errors.
- Network problems.
- System boot events.
Imagine your Spark application fails. Logs can help you identify whether the problem came from configuration, permissions, memory, or another issue.
Important log locations
Location	Purpose
/var/log/	Main Linux log directory
/var/log/syslog	General system messages on Ubuntu
/var/log/auth.log	Authentication and login-related events
/var/log/kern.log	Kernel-related messages, if configured

Inspect the directory:
ls -lah /var/log/


5. What is journalctl?
journalctl is used to view logs collected by the systemd journal.
Command	Purpose
journalctl	View journal logs
journalctl -n 50	Last 50 log entries
journalctl -f	Follow new logs live
journalctl -u ssh	Logs for SSH
journalctl -b	Logs from current boot
journalctl -p err	Show error-priority messages

Practical 3 – Read recent logs
journalctl -n 50


Show SSH service logs:
journalctl -u ssh -n 50


Follow logs live:
journalctl -f


Press Ctrl + C to exit live log viewing.
6. Real-world troubleshooting example
Suppose your Nginx website is not opening.

A Linux administrator can follow this process:
systemctl status nginx


If the service is failed, inspect its logs:
journalctl -u nginx -n 50


Check configuration:
sudo nginx -t


If the configuration test succeeds and you have made a valid change:
sudo systemctl restart nginx


Then verify:
systemctl status nginx


This is a basic service troubleshooting workflow.
